rmat just needs the single general principle as a markdown list item, I'll skip restating the validity check.\n\nI'm drafting the rule as one bullet with nested conditions: trigger when a span's head noun denotes a pathological condition or deficit state and serves as the result/complement of a predicate characterizing or causing a SpecificDisease mention, with coordinated conjuncts labeled the same way, plus a negative constraint excluding anatomical structures, genes/proteins, procedures, and non-condition physical consequences.\n\nI also want the negative constraint to exclude named disease nomenclature (SpecificDisease), pure disease-name modifiers, and merely locative/temporal adjunct frames rather than causal ones, while making clear that lexical familiarity alone never substitutes for the head-noun semantic test. I'm finalizing the wording now as a single markdown bullet with IF/THEN/negative-constraint sub-bullets.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Principle: Pathological-State Spans in Disease-Characterization Frames**\n  - **IF** a span, taken as a whole, denotes a pathological condition, clinical sign, symptom, lesion, or deficit state (its head is a state noun, or a non-state noun qualified by an abnormality-denoting modifier), **AND** the span is an argument of a predicate that characterizes, causes, or results from a SpecificDisease mention (or is a conjunct of such an argument in a coordinated list),\n  - **THEN** label the span `DiseaseClass`, and label each parallel conjunct in the same coordinated list that also meets the head-noun condition.\n  - **Negative constraint (do NOT apply when):**\n    - the head denotes an anatomical structure, a gene, a gene product or molecule, a substance or fluid, or a procedure, even inside a characterization frame;\n    - the span is itself disease nomenclature (an eponym or an \"X disease/syndrome/disorder\" name), which is `SpecificDisease`;\n    - the span is a bare modifier or a composite name that does